A||||23||JSocha||Any doctors or Diabetics that can shed some light? (LONG)||||8||
Z||000000||JSocha||06-13-2001||01:06 PM||||I'm hoping someone can shed some light on this subject of Diabities.<P>Last year around fall and at the request and pleading of my wife (as I was experiencing chest pains), I finally went in for a physical/exam and the doctor who I am seeing for the first time, who is originally from India, diagnoses me with having TYPE 2 Diabities where previous physicals and/or exams did not indicate or show a problem and my levels where they were normal.  <P>Was something that apparently happened just recently. <P>Talked to another doctor who I had an appointment for a Colonoscopy about two weeks later after the exam and during the preliminary meet he went through my past exams and asked me what had happened from Spring of 2000 to the recent test's in my diet that would cause the drastic shift in my sugar levels.<P>Informed him that I started drinking 4 - 6 liters a day of Mountain Dew shortly just shortly after my last exam.  Subsequently he told me to get off the MT. Dew as quickly as possible which I already had done after my first exam.<P>After that, I went in again for another appointment and the doctor (from India), didn't tell me not to eat and/or drink prior to the appointment, takes blood and sends it in to check the history 6 months back.<P>Takes a sugar level test there as well and I'm way over 200.  I informed him that I just ate  a 12" sub sandwich and a large Dt. Pepsi no more then 15 minutes prior to my appt. and if that would increase my sugar levels.  Answer is No.  <P>Run in to the other doctor who I have my colonoscopy appointment with and asked him if that would have an effect.  Definate YES is his reply.  Now I am at odds with the doctor I am seeing.<P>Go to MN with my wife whose father is a diabetic and has to test his sugar levels constantly with one of those electronic monitors.  So I get there and ask if it would be possible to monitor my sugar levels while I am there.  Lowest reading I had was a "62" and the highest I had during the entire weekend was "120" which about an hour or two later was down to around "70" and after sitting around not doing anything physical.<P>Come back from MN and I had an appointment with the Life Insurance nurse to take tests that Monday and haven't eaten since 6PM that Sunday evening when we got back from MN up until 4PM that Monday (as she was running late), takes tests and I mention about being diaganosed with TYPE 2 Diabities to answer one of her questions.  She takes her monitor out, calibrates it, tests my blood sugar level, "65" result.  Next morning (Tuesday) at 8AM I have an appointment for another blood test with the lab and then again with my doctor that afternoon.  Again, haven't eaten since Sunday or drank one drop of fluid.<P>Lab showed "172" (supposed to more accurate right from the vein) and the doc's shows "142" that afternoon (still haven't eaten or drank anything...and shaking).  Results back from the lab in Bismarck and my blood says I show that I have been a diabetic for well over 6 months.<P>Now from MN, to Monday to Tuesday...starving to death on top of it...the sugar levels go from what I was monitoring, the LI nurse read to their tests, I now tell the doctor I want to see a specialist.  So he sends me to an endocronogist in Minot (another guy from India mind you) along with copies of my medical records and who 5 minutes to noon after giving me an exam and then doing blood work which wasn't finished until then, pulls me into his office and tells me point blank "I'm not going to spend much time with you because I want to go for lunch" then starts prescribing me all kinds of medication and tells me I have diabities.  So I ask, the medication he is prescribing, can I eventually get off of it if I loose weight.  Tells me yes, prescribes one of those monitors as well and sends me out the door to which I ride the elevator with him and won't talk or answer questions because he is "officially on lunch".<P>Get back, I don't get the prescriptions filled only to find out later from someone that has Diabeties that the medication he prescribed is the worse medication there is and once you are on, you can never get off of it.  So I go to the pharmacist, same information.  On top of it, I later find out that before it should be prescribed that he should have run a test where they load me up with sugar and see how my blood cells are coated.<P>Go back to my doctor here in town and ask him to refer me to someone else.  Again, sends me to another guy from India who is a diabetic specialist who now has copies of my complete medical records from my doctor.  Again, TYPE 2 Diabetic. Prescribes me different medicication which I find out yes I can get off of if I loose weight which is confirmed.<P>Still not convinced, because I answered NO to most of both of their questions.  <BR>Do I get up frequently during the night to urinate?<BR>Does my vision become blurred?  (recently had my eyes checked...still healthy as an ox)<BR>Do I get thirsty or become dhydrated and find myself drinking more then I should?  (Hmmmm?  2 days without food and liquid...hungry...slightly thirsty...but not dhydrated)<BR>Do I become extremly fatigued during the day?  (I work mentally and sometimes physically running around fixing things here at work, moving heavy computers, then go home and work like a dog in the house and/or yard...yeah!)<BR>etc...<P>So I finally go and see another doctor, independently and out of town.  Sugar levels are extremly high, however, three enzymes (or hormones) on my liver are found to be extremely high as well...so they test me for Hepetitis, which thankfully comes out negative and could be what is affecting my sugar levels.  Get asked how much I drink, alchohol wise as apparently that is the only thing that can raise the enzymes on my liver if Hep is not the cause.  I'm lucky if I have a drink or two in a month and my wife can vouch for that is my "truthfull" answer.  Tells me I have to be an alcholoic and I must be and I'm in Denial.  So I ask him if being a Celiac (someone that is alergic to Oats, Wheat, Rye and Barley...anything that contains Gluton) has anything to do with it.  Never heard of it and didn't know and felt I wasn't being truthfull with him about a potential drinking problem I have.  Leave there "P" 'd off like I did the first specialist I was sent to!<P>Go to another specialist (this time from America) on my own, enzymes high on the liver, sugar levels normal (no TYPE 2 diagnosis), checked for Hepetitis again, negative.  Go to another specialist assigned by him...again American...sugar levels are high, liver enzymes high...asked to "cut down on" or "cut out completely" any bread or products containing gluten due to my liver enzymes being high which is affecting my sugar levels and due to the fact I have been a Celiac since childhood.  <P>So I go totally "CARB FREE", loosing weight like there was no tomorrow (from a size 44, down to a 42 for the longest time now, just bought pants this past weekend with a 40 waiste and now ready to fit into a size 38).  Anyways, prior to the waiste size and recent clothes purchase, I go back for another appointment with the most recent specialist...liver enzymes are down, way down (don't know the numbers) sugar levels are down around normal (after eating and drinking) and informed to stay away from the gluton products (which I told him I am...still running carb free) and I am "NOT" diabetic because he sent the first blood drawn in to be tested to some lab in MN and it came back negative on the 6 months test.  This was last week.<P>Have to go into the doctor here (not the one from India) and after lab (this morning at 7:30AM) cause I'm feeling down (wife has been sick with a bad, bad cold and my youngest was to and had a virus), get a blood test (again from the vein), they check my sugar levels at the same time (per doctors request) my sugar level was "313!", didn't eat or drink since 6PM lastnight and the hand held monitor was just under "300"!  Result...I'm back being diagnosed with TYPE 2 Diabities and they want to send me to their pal over in Minot, the first endocronolgist who didn't want to spend time with me because it was almost lunch!  Told them everything I have just told you and they don't believe me and somehow, I can't go see a specialist without a doctors recommendation first, yet I did once and got re-referred because they liver thing and being a Celiac was out of his expertise (didn't get charged either).  I didn't stay, told them to call the doctor I had just seen along with the specialist and I just walked out.  They just called me and said they were going over my medical records and there is no doubt I am TYPE 2 Diabetic.<P>Now I am fuming when I type this, so a lot of details have been left out (otherwise you would be reading a novel).  <P>However, can somebody give me some clue to "symptoms" one might experience if they have diabeties, besides blood test results and/or seeing a doctor?  No my feet don't ache, go numb and yes I can feel that damn little prickly wheel they run all the way up your leg and I can tell you where it pricks the most!  Eyesight is well over 20/20 (per optomotrist) w/some eye fatigue (because I stare into a computer monitor all day long).  I sleep all night from 10PM to 7AM, and depending on how big a meal I eat I may have eaten that evening, may get up none, once or twice to go to the bathroom.  Sugar was discovered in my urine here at the hospital, in Minot and the other guy from India, and the other places when it was above normal.<P>I am going to be making another appointment with another specialist in SD when I have some time off of work as I just learned another kid (close friends with his parents and I talked with his mom) was diagnosed with Diabities (again from the doctor here and in Minot who are from India that I got to see) only to find out later he had a blood infection and now after some damage was done due to the "Diabetic" medication he was prescribed to which he went into shock, ended up in the emergency room here and was loaded with more insulin medication before his parents grabbed him and rushed him to Fargo because they felt something was definately wrong.<P>Sorry to vent...yet I thank you for any information and/or advise you can give.  I just don't know which way is up anymore.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||8
Z||000001||84Bill||06-13-2001||01:30 PM||||Constant dry mouth or dry mouth<BR>Always running to the bathroom.<BR>Vision problems<P>BTW<BR>A fasting test is the kicker. If you dont eat anything the night before and skip breakfast before the test and your sugar levels are high chances are your diabetic But to what degree only a doctor can tell you. I must watch for the signs since my grandfather and father have diabetis I however do not (yet) I test my (fasting) sugar levels every once in a while and find it to be in the low double digits 50 or 60.<P>The best thing to do is gather information (educate yourself) and see several doctors as you have been doing. It's a pain in the ass but ya got to do it. <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||06-13-2001||01:46 PM||||email banditbalz@home.com  fellow fiero member... he know's it ALL!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||02:08 PM||||I cant answer any of your questions but I can tell you what I would be doing if I were in your situation. Id be learning everything about being diabetic that i can.<P>and then when the doctor or a test tells me something, i would make sure I understand whats going on and what it means, not simply taking someone elses word for it. <P>sounds like there is something going on, some interaction, with your diet restrictions, your liver and emzines... some other thing that most diabetics dont experience, that is throwing off the doctors.<P>but thats just me. If a doctor wants me to do something, or to take a medication, he has to explain it a least to some level so I understand what is going on.<P>  one other thing, i have no idea what it means to have your blood sugar level bouncing around from 50 to 300, i dont know what the normal ranges are, and when it becomes dangerous, but that is something I would get some understanding on real quick, and I would start checking it frequently to at least be able to tell the doc: heres where its been every day for the last week, month...||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||06-13-2001||02:22 PM||||JSocha:<P>drop me an email and we can talk about this sometime in the next couple of days.  It sounds like you've been given a lot of incorrect and contradicting information and unless I missed something (boy, you wrote a lot there!) a couple of things were missed. I'm also a type-II diabetic and from what I'm reading it doesn't sound like you're getting proper care. <P>A couple of things:<P>A blood sugar level of 62 is way low.  Anything below 80 is cause for concern, anything below 60 is potentially life-threatening and needs to be addresses quickly (a few ounces of orange juice does the trick). <P>Until the disease is under control ONLY an endocrinologist should be prescribing medication or changing dosage.  If you don't like the one you're seeing, go see another one.  Let them refer you to a primary care physician, not the other way around.  You're going to be seeing both doctors a lot the rest of your life, so pick someone you like and trust. <P>The test to check the sugar in your red blood cells is the 90 day glucose test.  Your body is constantly making new red blood cells and they live for about 90 days before wearing out and being destroyed, and the amount of sugar in your blood affects the way they are made in a way that can be measured.  The test has only been around a few years but its a much more accurate predictor than the old glucose tolerance test they used to give.  There is no '6 month' test.<P>Right now there are about 5 different medications available in the US to treat type-II diabetes.   Depending on your particular case one or another may be a better choice, depending on the root cause some drugs are better than others and the side effects differ as well. <P>Did you see an opthamologist for a retinal scan (where they dilate your pupils and check for damage)?  You shouldn't be seeing just an optometrist. Also did they check for protein in your urine (looking for kidney damage) and are they monitoring your chloresterol level? <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000005||baptistheart||06-13-2001||02:55 PM||||Check out web md it has almost every thing on it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||06-13-2001||03:14 PM||||Chilled down now.<P>When I went in for the chest pains, my cholestrol levels were very high (365 somthing-bad cholestrol and around a 100 good cholestrol...been several months since the first test and I'm a "milkaholic", but that has dropped as well since the last specialist).  <P>After my wife requested exam, they prescribed me some cholestrol medication to get my levels down and then found out that I "may be" TYPE 2 Diabetic.  They canceled the perscriptions and thats when this whole mess started whether or not I'm diabetic or not.  One doctor/specialist says yeah the others say no...who the F do I believe.<P>I was informed (by the doctors from India) anything above 120 is considered diabetic and the normal range is between 70 and 120 and will spike but go back down to normal after about 2 hours after eating.  Said the test that was conducted can check back roughly 6 months...don't know how.  One came back positive, other negative.<P>When I bottomed out at 62 in MN (and had absolutely no energy either), I hadn't eaten all day and was running around quite a bit at the mall with the wife shopping in Fargo that Saturday with her father.<P>I'm so fed up with doctors (especially the ones here in our town...but without running all over the country side...who else can I go to right away and still be able to work).  I also wonder if many of them should be even practicing.  Not like many years ago when they went to University, they actually had to meet a very strict standard and level.  Today, I feel people right out of the University just bought the right to say they are a doctor without any knowledge.  Seems the older they are, the more the check into things.  The younger, seems like you are on an assembly line.<P>Talked with one of our Sales reps from CO when he was up visiting our plant last year who is also a Diabetic.  He said I should go to Rochester, MN and get checked out versus messing around with these clowns around here.<P>84Bill - don't get dry mouth, even if I don't have anything to drink the entire day.  After two days though, get a tad bit more of the dry mouth symptoms.  However, if I drink lots during any particular day, go to the bathroom typically 5 to 6 times in an 8 hour day.<P>Fasted for two days, low test only to go to my Dr. appointment where it was unbelievably high.<P>From what I remember of the numbers:<P>With the last specialist I am seeing, first appointment, spent two days.  First thing in the morning (after not eating or drinking since 8PM the previous day) low 73; ate small breakfast with him (2 large milks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ), 117;  after an hour under 96 after 2 hours (both with no activity), 79.  Went out for lunch (chicken burger and fries and 2 LG Mt. Dews), 136; waited two hours, went shopping with lots of walking activity, went back to his office, 77.<P>Large supper that evening with him (Steak & lobster, baked potatoe, dinner roll, cheese cake and Large Mt. Dew  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ), 143.  After about an hour sitting at the restraunt visiting, 112; went back to his office after about 2 1/2 hours of sitting in the restraunt, 84.  <P>Went swimming that night, didn't eat or drink (except for some bad pool water).  Next day, 6AM sharp before leaving town, blood test taken, result, 63;<P>Been watching the carbs.<P>If I could just find one doctor/specialist who has some brains, isn't a gazillion miles away and that I can trust, I wouldn't be posting this thread here.  Its just who do you believe anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||06-13-2001||03:29 PM||||JSocha<BR>Hehe yeah that don't sound bad to me and yes Larry 50 sugar was very low however I was quite hungry at the time and while eating 2 doughnuts my mother insisted I take the quick test  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>My coworker sitting in front of me is diabetic and for kicks and giggles we just tested me and I'm happy to report it was 168. I had lunch about 45 minutes ago.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||frontal lobe||06-13-2001||03:57 PM||||I will post later (probably much) tonight when I get time.  I am at work right now and much too busy accidentally killing people (joking, see "Let's get those..." thread).<p>[This message has been edited by frontal lobe (edited 06-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||LarryB||06-13-2001||04:21 PM||||100-something was probably your triglycerides figure.  A healthy reading for HDL (good) chloresterol is from 35 on up.<P>In any case it sounds like the doctors you saw need to lay off the craq pipe.  They sure don't sound like they know too much about how to diagnose or manage diabetes.   <P>The figures to alert to possible diabetes have been changing the last 5 years or so.  At one point a fasting level of 140 was cause for alarm, then it was 120, now its 110.  In any case, all they really mean is that you may need additional tests to see whats going on.   That's why they have the 90 day test; in my case they want me to keep the results of that test under 7.0. It's also possible you're what is known as 'glucose intolerant', which means you may be on the slippery slope but if you take care of yourself (if you're overweight, lose the gut) and eat better you'll hold your own.<P>Your blood sugar also naturally rises in the morning. Your internal clock tells your liver to boost your blood sugar levels every day around 4-5AM to get you ready for the day. If you're diabetic and you eat late at night you'll get higher readings in the morningbecause you don't metabolize as much sugar in the bloodstream when you're sleeping.<P>What you eat also affects how you spike.  If you eat carbohydrates (starch or sugar) your blood sugar level will spike quickly and reach a peak 1-2 hours after you eat, and should be near normal about 4 hours later.  If its protein with few carbs the spike will come somewhat later and be less intense. <P>And - your numbers look really good to me.  I'm lucky if my sugar levels are less than 180 2 hours after I eat a normal meal. (They taper off to normal levels 4 hours after I eat if I follow my diet and eat when I'm supposed to.)<P>You do need to check around and ditch Dr. Quack pronto.  Even if you have to drive 100 miles each way its worth it. <P>||||reg||4
Z||000010||JSocha||06-13-2001||04:23 PM||||Is one of the signs "irritability", cause I just got off the phone with my doctor who saw me this morning before dinner, wondering why I walked out before he was done and my blood pressure is back up.<P>Duh!<P>Told him that I'm not impressed with the doctors at Johnsons Clinic, that they are a bunch of morons and I wouldn't bring my dead dog to em because they would problably tell me to take my dog home, feed him and in a few days he should be as good as new.<P>July 17, 2000 first appointment:<BR>Height:  5'11"<BR>Weight:  305 lbs<P>August 9, 2000:<BR>Height:  same<BR>Weight:  285 (steady at following appointments with JC)<P>Today, June 13, 2001:<BR>Height:  same (at least something is consistant)<BR>Weight:  214 lbs<P>Asked him if the following could have led to the result of the 313 reading:<P>1)  Stopped carbs, loosing weight since<BR>2) Bought an 18 speed 26" bicycle on Saturday and Monday afternoon, peddled it to work for the first time and part way home before wife picked me up to run out and check on our farm as a storm went through.  Later that evening, went out again on our bikes.<BR>3)  Tuesday afternoon when weather was predictably nicer, rode it a little over 1 mile back to work and walked the rest of the distance.<BR>4) Tuesday evening, walked just under 4/10ths of a mile, rode bike the remaining mile section, walked the remaining distance (estimating 1/4 of a mile until tonight when I actually can measure with the odometer the respective distances) to my house from the interesection where I got off my bike and then did "gator crawls" and "Crab walks" for about an hour and a half in my yard up and down a 45 degree inclined hill.<P>Could my body be buring the fat, turning it back into sugar for the required energy, thus raising the test level to 313.<P>Answer:  He doesn't believe that would have done it and believes it is diabities.<P>Hmmmm?  From 305/285 pounds to 214 (edited typo) pounds?  Size 44 to a long time 42 wearer, to a recent 40 and ready to slip into a waiste size of 38?<P>I just bought size 40's this weekend which were "semi" but not quite tight around the waiste.  Put on one of the other pants I tried on and I can slip them over my hips without unbuttoning or unzipping them.a<P>Isn't fat just a medium of stored sugar?  I thought that is what they taught us in Biology classes when I was in High School and at University.  Or am I wrong?<P>Frontal Lobe - Sorry if the other thread about the "Doctors" insulted you any.  Got it last night in an email from one of my classmates who lives in MN and sends me jokes all the time and I thought I would share it here on the forum and then today all this comes up.  Bad, but somehow appropriate timing considering the circumstances I am going through.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 06-13-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000011||frontal lobe||06-13-2001||04:53 PM||||No, please, don't worry at all about me being offended.  I thought it was very funny.  I was just joking.  <P>I'll give you your answers tonight when I get a chance at home.  Do you want it on the forum so all can be educated, or do you want it by e-mail for privacy purposes?||||reg||1
Z||000012||JSocha||06-13-2001||05:00 PM||||Frontal, here is fine, doesn't bother me and just in case somebody else may be going through the same thing I am currently going through which is slowly "P"ing me off with the Doctors here and their golfing buddy specialists they had sent me to.<P>And "Anonymous", if you are a member of the forum and/or just reading posts, thanks for the email.  My step daughter forwarded it to me here at work from home and thought I would be interested.  Will check out <A HREF="http://www.diabetic.com" TARGET=_blank>www.diabetic.com</A>  tonight for information.  Just too "P"-d off to look now.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||06-13-2001||08:25 PM||||Hey JSocha<BR>Sounds like you have a winning plan going there. I just wanted to acknowledge your weight loss and diet and exercise plan, congratulations. Most people will deny they have a problem even when told they do and not do anything about it. In your case your not even sure yet and you have taken steps anyway.  Very smart move! I too have implemented steps to be sure I stay in realitivly good shape since I have problems in my history.<P>Keep it up even if there is not a problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||JSocha||06-13-2001||11:33 PM||||The exercise program...<P>Wanted to get into shape so I can get back into the wet suit, have a little more stamina so when the waters warm up a bit, I can go scuba diving and conserve air at the same time.<P>Also, I found out last week a friend of mine and his wife are back in town and he wants to get together this summer and work out and get his speed techniques up and teach me his martial arts style and to assist me in training for an upcoming rematch with a guy here in town just because 5 or so years ago I pinned him in less then 3 seconds in a submission technique (didn't hurt him) he couldn't get out of when we were goofing around.  <P>He's now a second degree black belt (when he was just a black belt when this happened) in Taekwondo and even though we were just goofing around for fun then, he apparently took it more personal then I thought because I was and still am only a high yellow belt in the style I was practicing at the time, Amarajutsu...which our slogan we went by was Real training for real life.<P>He wants to challenge me now during a demo he wants to give this summer and said I wouldn't be able to do it again now since he is higher in rank status.  <P>Talked with the instructor he works with here in town and told him I'm not interested in the challenge or the demo.  <P>Found out he apparently has had a really big chip on his shoulder since then and the instructor he works with wants me to take him down a notch, if I can since he has been unable to and thinks this whole thing is nonsense to begin with, which I agree.<P>Hense, Gator Crawls, Crab Walks...tonight was Shiko Wasa and standing leg squats (legs are sore as hell now from both) after a good session of stretching.  Hams hurt like heck now to.<P>I'm hoping that in the meantime, he drops this whole ridiculous rematch thing.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||06-14-2001||01:29 AM||||OK, guys, brace yourselves for a novel.  Sorry in advance.<P>First before I start-and I hate to do this because I would rather be thought of as lobe, the Fiero fan-here are my credentials:<P>U. of Iowa pharmacy school grad 1981<BR>U. of Illinois medical school 1985<BR>Internal medicine residency at Medical College of Wisconsin in Milwaukee grad 1988<BR>Private internal medicine practice 1988 to now.<BR>Assistant professor at the Medical College since 1988<BR>Clinical research trials with many products currently out on the U.S. market since 1993<BR>Currently doing diabetes trials now<P>I just did that to be fair to you so you can trust the following.<P>Type I diabetes-you HAVE to use insulin injections or you will die in a short period of time.<P>Type II diabetes-you MIGHT have to use insulin, but often will not need to.<P>Cause of type II:  gradually decreasing insulin levels AND decreased sensitivity of the tissues to insulin (the insulin doesn't work as well).  Complicated if there is "too much of you" for the insulin you can produce to "go around".<P>In most labs, 70-110 is normal.  Most people can usually at least maintain consciousness until the sugar gets down to the low 40's.  By the way, consider glucose a synonym for sugar.<P>A FASTING sugar of &gt;140 is automatically considered diabetes.  Fasting sugars of 110-140 are technically not diagnosed as diabetes, but practically means you are headed that way and although you wouldn't need medicine treatment, you should start eating and exercising like you are one.<P>A sugar of &gt;200 2 hours after a usual meal is considered diabetes.<P>It's important to remember that like a car engine has different tachometer readings throughout the day depending on what the car is doing, your body has different sugar readings hour to hour and day to day as well.  That is why some of your readings were good and some were bad.  In ALL diabetics, illnesses-even the common cold-make the sugar go up.<P>In order to get an over-all idea of what the sugars have been, there is an ingenious test called the glycohemoglobin, or another name is the hemoglobin a1c (hgbA1c).  Turns out they can perform a blood test that detects about how much sugar is "coating" the surface of a red blood cell and that it correlates with about how the sugars have been.  Since the average red blood cell has a life span of about 3 months, the hemoglobin a1c gives a more over-all picture of how the sugars have been then a simple blood sugar test, which is like a "snapshot" of that moment.  THE HEMOGLOBIN A1C TEST DOES NOT NEED TO BE DONE FASTING.  IT DOESN'T CHANGE IT.  Whereas, whether you are fasting or not has a huge difference on the blood sugar.<P>Regarding symptoms of diabetes, MOST patients are not going to have the symptoms you describe until the blood sugars are over 200.  And many of the symptoms you describe happen LATE in the course of the disease--and you don't want to get to that point.  So just FORGET going by any symptoms--go by monitoring your blood sugars, and by the every 3 month hemoglobin a1c test.<P>People that have elevated blood sugars ALMOST ALWAYS have elevated "bad" cholesterols, low "good" cholesterols, and elevated triglycerides.  The FIRST treatment I ALWAYS do for treating the cholesterol levels, is get the sugars under good control.  Often that is good enough to lower levels to where anti-cholesterol medicines are not necessary or if necessary, at much lower doses.<P>You have done a PHENOMENAL job of losing weight and exercising.  Congratulations.  This far dwarfs anything a doctor could do for you.  It GREATLY reduces the amount of insulin you need your body to produce, AND increases your tissue's sensitivity to insulin making what you do produce more efficient.<P>However, if your sugars are still CONSISTENTLY above 140, and your hemoglobin a1c above say 7.5, it would still be best to go on some sort of medicine.<P>Regarding medicines, THERE IS NO DIABETES MEDICINE THAT ONCE YOU START IT, YOU MUST CONTINUE IT.  In that, the medicine doesn't make it that now you are dependent on it.  Remember, though, that the natural course over time is for the pancreas to gradually produce less insulin as we age, no matter what we do.<P>Visual example:  my hair has quit producing pigment and so is gray.  If I start using Grecian Formula, do I then "have to use it for the rest of my life and can never stop it?"  Well, no, but if I stop it, my hair will go back to what it was and maybe worse since it may have grayed more while I was using the hair coloring.<P>So I would like to know what this "horrible" medicine was that once you are on it, you can't get off.  And btw, there was no test he needed to do before you started it.  Let me know what the medicines were that were considered for you and I will let you know about them.  There are others on the forum that could too, like the expert RossT who I thought also is in North Dakota.  Now that I think about it, he may know some excellent endocrinologists in North Dakota, too.<P>Now just to clear things up, yes, you DO have type II diabetes.  You always will from now on.  However, you MAY NOT need any medicine treatment for it.  You may have properly and adequately treated it with the dietary and lifestyle changes you have made.  But like LarryB said, you NEED to hook up with an endocrinologist that you can trust, work with, and communicate with because you are in this for the long haul.  You may have to try a few, but once you have one you can work with, you are set.<P>Just a few words about doctors.  The medical school I went to had about 300 in the class.  At U. of Ill. we had a special name we gave to the lowest ranking member of the group who just barely scraped by and passed:  Doctor.  The point is, even the worst guy who squeeked by gets called the same thing as the top ones in the class.  So if you are sensing that your doctor isn't doing a good job for you, then fire him and get someone who will.  Just like there is a wide difference in ability of mechanics, there is in doctors, too.<P>One other thing.  You will have celiac sprue your entire life also, and so you need to find a good gastroenterologist who you will not have to see very often but who probably once a year can follow along and see how you are doing and how the liver is doing.<P>Well, sorry about the LONG post, but I was trying to give you the concise but pretty complete picture instead of you having to try to search all over the internet and library and get your answers.  You will possibly have some questions after all this, so fire away.  But try to get them in soon if you want a quick response because I have to go out of town Fri to Sun to a meeting about starting another clinical drug trial, so wouldn't be able to get back to you until Monday.<P>Sorry in advance about any typo's, but I am too lazy right now to go back and proof read my own discourse.  It's pretty bad when you bore YOURSELF!||||reg||1
Z||000016||JSocha||06-14-2001||02:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>You have done a PHENOMENAL job of losing weight and exercising.  Congratulations.  This far dwarfs anything a doctor could do for you.  It GREATLY reduces the amount of insulin you need your body to produce, AND increases your tissue's sensitivity to insulin making what you do produce more efficient.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I understand this concept.  Taking for instance the body producing a shot glass of insulin to treat lets say a gallon for most physically fit people with out diabeties.  And the same shot glass to somebody with an equivelant 10 to 15 gallon body, such as myself.  The same shot has to try and work and compensate for the additional 9 to 14 gallons and get the body to the same level.<P>Just confused that my sugar levels are all over the place (always worse when taken at the Rugby clinic) and then one group says I am the other says I'm not.  <P>At some point I have just reached the level where I don't know who to believe anymore.<P>The doctors who I seen and who are from India are convinced I am and didn't take that much time at all with me.  In and out the door as fast as they could.  Busy lecturing, perscribing medications and not listing to me their patient or willing to answer any questions or explain it clearly, if they even could.  <P>Dr. Fahar out of Minot and President of I believe the ND Endocronolgy board (???) would ask me a question and wouldn't even listen to the answer.  He is also the one when my blood work was finished 5 minutes to lunch hour, told me point blank he didn't want to spend to much time with me cause he wanted to go to lunch.  Then when we rode the elevator down together, I asked him a question and he told me "I'm on my lunch hour now".  Real impressive to me, especially when he wanted me back in two weeks, to which I canceled that appointment because he just teed me off so bad because of his attitude towards me.  I was cooperative and he was an utter @ss in my book.  The American's so-so to not diabetic.<P>Oh, and was also told by the second from India specialist I am a "Border-line Diabetic".<P>Is there such a thing as a Border-Line Diabetic?  To me that is like saying one is only "half pregnant".  To me, you either are or you are not ... no in between.<P>I will look for and dig up the prescription he had wrote and post it here along with some of the related pamphlet literature titles he had sent home with me.||||reg||1
