Z||000019||JSocha||06-14-2001||09:25 AM||||Ross, approximately 50+ pounds.  WoooHooo! &lt;happy camper here&gt;<P>Also I'm realizing that I'm nothing but muscle under all that lost fat!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Gettin rid of them ole "bucket handles" and replacing them with good ole "love handles"!<P>Thanks again Frontal! - Good information, some that was not explained to me.  <P>I guess I will just need to find somebody closer who takes some time with me (and just doesn't try gettin me out the door for his next case), listens and takes an interest and is willing to explain things better.  The others didn't.  <P>Went in for another lab test this morning on the blood w/a different doctor.<P>Have an appointment with him tomorrow late afternoon.<P>Wife and I got into an argument (just a small btub spat) over this Diabities thing lastnight.<P>I just let her know some say I am while the others say I am not.  And all I need to know is just who is more credible or which side to listen to cause I don't want to be treated for something I could not have had like my wife was.  Where she was treated for "Sinus Infection" when she never had it but had allergys and asthma.  Dr. here prescribed her medication she didn't need.<P>All she did was get worse and never seemed to get better.  So they kept perscribing her different, stronger medication until I told her to make an appointment with a specialist in Bismarck.  He took her off of a majority of the medicines that were perscribed.<P>I often wonder how many people have been diagnosed and treated for something they "did" not have due to Dr. error and/or a misdiagnosis?<p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000020||frontal lobe||06-14-2001||10:39 AM||||JSocha, don't get too hung up on the semantics (wording).  The strict definitions would put you as a type II diabetic without any doubt, even considering the conflicting, incomplete, and misleading information you have been given.  A "border-line diabetic" would be someone who falls into that range between normal (up to 110) and STRICTLY in the diabetes range (above 140).  So border-line diabetics would have fasting sugars of 110-140.<P>Since you have treated your type II diabetes by yourself without medicines (which is awesome-way to go!), your sugars will probably fall into the 110-140 range much of the time.  Does that mean you aren't "diabetic" anymore?  Are you now a "borderline diabetic"?  Semantics.  Actually the strict classification is that you would be a diet and exercise CONTROLLED type II diabetic.<P>It's really practically irrelevant what label you put on yourself regarding this.  The main implication of the semantics is for insurance purposes more than medical purposes.  What IS relevant is what are your sugars and hemoglobin a1c's and what medicines if any are you needing to control it.  And is your body showing any effects of damage.  The earliest concerning finding would be what LarryB mentioned:  protein in the urine.<P>Regarding how often a misdiagnosis occurs--about as often as you take your car in and the mechanics misdiagnose things.  Some problems in both fields are hard to diagnose.  Some are easy.  Your case was so easy I could do it over the internet several states away.  You just didn't get your questions and concerns answered properly, through no fault of your own.<P>Regarding people being president of the endocrinology board of ND, etc.  Those political-type positions seem to impress patients, but in reality those doctors are often more politicians and less good clinicians, so don't put much weight on that.<P>To 84Bill, keep the check!  If I take a check for THAT information, then I'll have to start sending checks out to all the kind people on the forum here who have freely given so much information about Fiero's.  I am glad to help in an area where I have some experience.<P>So after all this, WHAT DID THE BLOOD TEST SHOW TODAY???||||reg||1
Z||000021||JSocha||06-14-2001||10:56 AM||||WHAT DID THE BLOOD TEST SHOW TODAY???<P>I won't know until tomorrow afternoon when I go in for my Dr.'s appointment.<P>I'm still wonderin about that 313 level they told me about yesterday.<P>Talked with my other Dr. this morning (he finally called me back) who I saw last week, the only thing he could surmize is somebody may have goofed in the lab on the test and recommened I have another one performed immidately (already done).  <P>Also is going to give me a list of Dr.'s closer to me that he feels I will be comfortable with and qualified so I don't have to run down to Fargo all the time to see him and don't have to mess with the bozo's here at our clinic.<P>He will also be faxing me over an article about how to find the right doctor as well and some questions to ask either myself and/or the doctor to see if they will be right for me.||||reg||1
